From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 04:27:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: apple-soc: read the T8132 current state from
 the status register

On T8132 the driver reports the requested P-state, as the status
register's layout was not known for this SoC. On a J604 the P cluster
does not always run at what it was asked for: with state 20 (4464 MHz)
requested, a single busy thread runs at 3.93-3.99 GHz by its cycle
counter, yet cpuinfo_cur_freq says 4464000.

The status word read through dvfs_registers shows the T8112 layout,
target in bits 4:0 and current state in bits 9:5: with 20 requested the
P cluster reports 20 when idle, 16 (3984 MHz) with one busy thread and
15 (3936 MHz) with two or four, which matches the cycle counter. The
efficiency cluster reports its requested 8 as current, with bit 31 set
while a change is in progress.

Use that layout for T8132, so the hardware frequency the cpufreq core
reads is the one the cluster runs at.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/cpufreq/apple-soc-cpufreq.c | 10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/apple-soc-cpufreq.c b/drivers/cpufreq/apple-soc-cpufreq.c
index 1dea507720..e6de0f032a 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/apple-soc-cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/apple-soc-cpufreq.c
@@ -326,15 +326,19 @@ static const struct apple_soc_cpufreq_info soc_t8152_info = {
 };
 
 /*
- * T8132 uses 5-bit PS1 state IDs and no PS2. The command register reads back
- * the last request: the status register's current-state field is not
- * established on this SoC. States above 4 (P 1800 / E 1860 MHz) are held back
+ * T8132 uses 5-bit PS1 state IDs and no PS2. The status register has the
+ * T8112 layout, target in bits 4:0 and current state in bits 9:5, measured on
+ * a J604: with state 20 requested, the P cluster reports 20 while idle, 16
+ * with one busy thread and 15 with two or four, which matches the 3.93-3.99
+ * GHz the busy core's cycle counter shows. States above 4 (P 1800 / E 1860 MHz) are held back
  * until the board thermal policy takes ownership, see
  * apple_soc_cpufreq_set_thermal_owner(). This bootstrap ceiling is an existing
  * Linux restriction, not a qualified firmware thermal or safety limit.
  */
 static const struct apple_soc_cpufreq_info soc_t8132_info = {
 	.needs_thermal_owner = true,
+	.cur_pstate_mask = APPLE_DVFS_STATUS_CUR_PS_T8112,
+	.cur_pstate_shift = APPLE_DVFS_STATUS_CUR_PS_SHIFT_T8112,
 	.max_unmanaged_pstate = 4,
 	.max_pstate = 31,
 	.ps1_mask = APPLE_DVFS_CMD_PS1,
